  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies MLP and Transformer learning on synthetic k-parity data and cites grokking as context, but it does not measure delayed generalization after training-accuracy saturation or test whether that delay grows as the training set shrinks.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports its own k-parity train/test accuracy and data-size optimization results, recorded below as related findings. Those endpoint results do not establish a delayed test-accuracy rise after overfitting. Its references to grokking are contextual; cited results from other work are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper studies MLP and minGPT learning of noisy k-parity under fixed and dataset-scaled budgets. It reports endpoint train/test accuracies and an optimisation-slowdown effect of data diversity. Grokking appears only as a cited example of phenomena studied with synthetic tasks. No result reports test accuracy rising long after training saturation, delay versus dataset size, circuit formation, weight-norm or initialisation interventions, or optimiser or numerical artifacts.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct but incomplete. The authors also conclude that the Transformers' stronger simplicity bias makes them robust to noisy features but poor at learning complex, low-availability features. MLPs show the opposite pattern and scale better with data on complex features.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper does not reproduce the original grokking method; it studies MLPs and a minGPT variant on noisy k-parity data with fixed and dataset-scaled iteration budgets.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported optimization and accuracy outcomes vary with training-set size and budget regime (fixed versus T = 1.25N); the paper also reports architecture-dependent outcomes.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Under a fixed budget of T=10^4, GPT on d=18 k-parity overfits at small N (train 0.993, test 0.537 at N=2,400). It undertrains at large N (train 0.506 at N=80,000). N=8,000 is best (test 0.757).",
        "proposed_claim": "At a fixed iteration budget, greater data diversity slows Transformer optimisation on k-parity, so a smaller or intermediate training set performs better.",
        "row_ids": [
          "fixed_d18_N2400_train",
          "fixed_d18_N2400_test",
          "fixed_d18_N8000_train",
          "fixed_d18_N8000_test",
          "fixed_d18_N24000_train",
          "fixed_d18_N24000_test",
          "fixed_d18_N80000_train",
          "fixed_d18_N80000_test",
          "fixed_d18_Ninf_train",
          "fixed_d18_Ninf_test"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "With T=1.25N, more data extends the learnable-feature frontier (d=18: test 1.000 at N≥80,000). At d=22, however, train accuracy is 0.904 at N=24,000 versus 0.771 at N=240,000, despite ten times more iterations.",
        "proposed_claim": "The diversity-induced optimisation slowdown in Transformers on k-parity persists even when passes per example are held constant.",
        "row_ids": [
          "scaled_d18_N2400_train",
          "scaled_d18_N2400_test",
